One-way functions would imply \(\mathrm{P}
e \mathrm{NP}\), since \(\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}\) would permit efficient search for a polynomial-length preimage. Whether \(\mathrm{P}
e \mathrm{NP}\) alone implies their existence is separately recorded in TCS-0022. One-way functions are equivalent to several basic cryptographic primitives, including pseudorandom generators and suitable private-key encryption, but it is not known whether they suffice for public-key encryption; that implication is TCS-6545. The present question assumes neither \(\mathrm{P}
e \mathrm{NP}\) nor any candidate’s hardness.
